{%- comment -%}
  The studio illustration, hand-authored as real vector paths.

  Every export the merchant had was a PNG wrapped in an <svg>, which is what
  image generators and most "save as SVG" buttons produce. A bitmap has no
  parts, so nothing inside it can be animated, recoloured or scaled — this
  exists so it can be all three.

  Nothing here is a hex value. Fills and strokes are set in CSS from the
  brand tokens, so changing the palette in theme settings repaints the
  drawing. That is the whole reason this is markup and not an image.

  Stroked paths carry pathLength="100". It makes the drawing animation
  exact and resolution-independent: dasharray 100 / dashoffset 100 is a
  fully undrawn stroke and 0 is a finished one, whatever the real length of
  the curve happens to be.

  Accepts: alt {String} — describes the artwork. Blank means decorative.
{%- endcomment -%}

<svg
  class="studio-art"
  viewBox="0 0 480 300"
  preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
  fill="none"
  {% if alt != blank %}
    role="img" aria-label="{{ alt | escape }}"
  {% else %}
    aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
  {% endif %}
>
  {%- comment -%} ---------- Bee, wings, face and brush ---------- {%- endcomment -%}
  <g class="art-bee">
    {%- comment -%}
      The brush first, so it sits behind the body — it is held, not laid on
      top. Handle, ferrule, then the loaded tip.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="art-brush">
      <path class="art-ink" d="M 168 176 L 292 242" stroke-width="15" stroke-linecap="round" />
      <path class="art-ink" d="M 288 240 L 306 250" stroke-width="21" stroke-linecap="round" />
      <path class="art-ink-fill" d="M 304 247 C 326 242, 344 254, 336 268 C 327 283, 296 275, 292 259 Z" />
    </g>

    {%- comment -%}
      Wings are outlines rather than filled shapes, matching the drawn look.
      Both start at the same root so they hinge where a wing would.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="art-bee__wings">
      <path
        class="art-ink art-wing art-wing--upper"
        d="M 154 128 C 122 78, 58 66, 46 100 C 34 134, 104 150, 154 128 Z"
        stroke-width="7"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
        pathLength="100"
      />
      <path
        class="art-ink art-wing art-wing--lower"
        d="M 152 136 C 128 116, 84 112, 78 132 C 72 154, 122 156, 152 136 Z"
        stroke-width="7"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
        pathLength="100"
      />
    </g>

    {%- comment -%}
      The abdomen and its stripes share one rotation, so the stripes stay
      square to the body instead of being redrawn at an angle. The clip keeps
      them inside the ellipse.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="art-bee__body" transform="rotate(-34 128 168)">
      <clipPath id="bloom-art-abdomen">
        <ellipse cx="128" cy="168" rx="58" ry="42" />
      </clipPath>
      <ellipse class="art-body" cx="128" cy="168" rx="58" ry="42" />
      <g clip-path="url(#bloom-art-abdomen)">
        <rect class="art-stripe" x="70" y="120" width="20" height="96" />
        <rect class="art-stripe" x="104" y="120" width="17" height="96" />
        <rect class="art-stripe" x="136" y="120" width="17" height="96" />
      </g>
    </g>

    <circle class="art-body" cx="190" cy="118" r="46" />

    <g class="art-bee__face">
      <path class="art-ink" d="M 165 113 Q 174 101 183 113" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round" />
      <path class="art-ink" d="M 197 113 Q 206 101 215 113" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round" />
      <path class="art-ink" d="M 172 130 Q 190 151 208 130" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round" />
      <ellipse class="art-blush" cx="159" cy="133" rx="10" ry="6.5" />
      <ellipse class="art-blush" cx="221" cy="133" rx="10" ry="6.5" />
    </g>

    {%- comment -%}
      Each antenna is its own group so it can draw on and then wobble about
      the point where it meets the head.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="art-antenna art-antenna--left">
      <path class="art-ink" d="M 176 77 C 170 53, 178 37, 192 31" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round" pathLength="100" />
      <circle class="art-ink-fill" cx="196" cy="29" r="9" />
    </g>
    <g class="art-antenna art-antenna--right">
      <path class="art-ink" d="M 200 74 C 206 51, 222 41, 237 38" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round" pathLength="100" />
      <circle class="art-ink-fill" cx="241" cy="37" r="9" />
    </g>
  </g>

  {%- comment -%}
    ---------- The heart ----------

    Two strokes, not a filled heart. Both start at the bottom point, where the
    brush is, and sweep up and over their own lobe — so drawing them from
    dashoffset 100 to 0 reads as the brush painting them, in the order a hand
    would.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <path
    class="art-heart art-heart--left"
    d="M 368 232 C 328 202, 300 164, 311 136 C 321 112, 355 112, 368 136"
    stroke-width="26"
    stroke-linecap="round"
    pathLength="100"
  />
  <path
    class="art-heart art-heart--right"
    d="M 368 232 C 408 202, 436 164, 425 136 C 415 112, 381 112, 368 136"
    stroke-width="26"
    stroke-linecap="round"
    pathLength="100"
  />

  {%- comment -%} ---------- Leaf sprig ---------- {%- endcomment -%}
  <g class="art-sprig">
    <path class="art-stem" d="M 374 282 C 402 274, 432 262, 462 240" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round" pathLength="100" />
    {%- comment -%}
      The rotation sits on a wrapper, never on the leaf itself. The leaf is
      scaled by CSS, and an element carrying both a transform attribute and an
      animated CSS transform loses the attribute.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <g transform="rotate(-20 392 288)"><ellipse class="art-leaf" cx="392" cy="288" rx="15" ry="8" /></g>
    <g transform="rotate(-62 407 258)"><ellipse class="art-leaf art-leaf--light" cx="407" cy="258" rx="14" ry="7.5" /></g>
    <g transform="rotate(-24 421 277)"><ellipse class="art-leaf" cx="421" cy="277" rx="15" ry="8" /></g>
    <g transform="rotate(-62 436 248)"><ellipse class="art-leaf art-leaf--light" cx="436" cy="248" rx="14" ry="7.5" /></g>
    <g transform="rotate(-30 449 266)"><ellipse class="art-leaf" cx="449" cy="266" rx="15" ry="8" /></g>
  </g>
</svg>
